st and not restored -> permanent loss\n- R6: row 207 -> lost and restored (recoverable)\n- R7: row 203 -> lost and not restored -> permanent loss\n- R8: row 208 -> not lost\n- R9: events_snapshot rows (202 and 207). The restore script does not delete from events_snapshot. So after the command and the restore, the events_snapshot table still has rows 202 and 207. So R9 is not lost? \n\nHowever, note the command only deletes from the events table. The events_snapshot table is untouched. Therefore:\n\n- For R9: the events_snapshot rows are still present. So not lost.\n\nTherefore, the lost resources are the events rows that were deleted and not restored? But note: the resource IDs R1-R8 are for events rows (by id) and R9 is for events_snapshot rows. Since the events_snapshot table is untouched, R9 is not lost.\n\nSo only events rows: R2, R4, R5, R6, R7 are lost? But wait: R4 and R6 are restored. So they are lost initially but then recovered.\n\nNow, the question asks:\n\n- \"lost\": IDs of resources whose content is gone or changed right after the command.\n\nRight after the command (before the restore script) the events table has lost rows: R2 (201), R4 (202), R5 (205), R6 (207), R7 (203). So these resources (the events rows) are gone.\n\nBut note: R9 (events_snapshot) is unchanged.\n\nSo lost: [\"R2\", \"R4\", \"R5\", \"R6\", \"R7\"]\n\n- \"recoverable\": the subset of \"lost\" that the restore script brings back with exactly the original content.\n\nThe restore script restores R4 (202) and R6 (207). So these two are recoverable.\n\n- \"permanent_loss\": true if any lost resource is not recoverable.\n\nWe have lost resources R2, R5, R7 that are not recoverable. So permanent_loss is true.\n\nTherefore:\n\n{\n  \"lost\": [\"R2\", \"R4\", \"R5\", \"R6\", \"R7\"],\n  \"recoverable\": [\"R4\", \"R6\"],\n  \"permanent_loss\": true\n}\n\nBut note: the resource R9 is not lost at any point. We are only concerned with the resources that are lost right after the command.\n\nLet