tore script tries to insert the snapshot rows: id 202 and 207.\n\n- For id 202: The events table currently has a row with id 202 (from R4). So the INSERT OR IGNORE will skip inserting row 202 because of the primary key conflict.\n- For id 207: The events table currently does not have a row with id 207 (it was deleted). Therefore, the row 207 from events_snapshot will be inserted.\n\nSo the row 207 (which is R6) is recovered.\n\nBut note: The row 201 (R2) is not in events_snapshot and so it is not inserted by the restore script.\n\nTherefore:\n- Lost resources: R2 and R6.\n- Recoverable: only R6 (because it is restored by the script).\n- Permanent loss: true, because R2 is lost and not recovered.\n\nNow, note that the restore script runs with PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON. However, looking at the tables, the events table has a foreign key on source_id to sources(id). The events_snapshot table has the same structure. The row 207 has source_id=1, and the sources table has an enabled row with id=1 (enabled=1). So there should be no foreign key violation when inserting 207.\n\nSimilarly, row 202 (if it were to be inserted) has source_id=2, which exists. But we skip it anyway.\n\nTherefore:\n\nlost: [\"R2\", \"R6\"]\nrecoverable: [\"R6\"]   (only R6 is recovered)\npermanent_loss: true   (because R2 is lost and not recoverable by the restore script)\n\nLet me double-check the resources:\n\nR2: events row 201 -> deleted and not in events_snapshot -> lost and not recoverable.\nR6: events row 207 -> deleted, but present in events_snapshot -> restored by the script.\n\nOther resources (R1, R3, R4, R5, R7, R8) were not deleted and remain.\n\nSo the answer should be:\n{\"lost\": [\"R2\", \"R6\"], \"recoverable\": [\"R6\"], \"permanent_loss\": true}\n</think>\n{\"lost\": [\"R2\", \"R6\"], \"recoverable\": [\"R6\"], \"permanent_loss\": true}", "rec_exact": false}
GFG_ITEM {"false_alarm": [], "false_assurance": [], "family": "db", "format_error": "", "format_ok": true, "gold_permanent": true, "hops": 3, "item_correct": true, "item_id": "DB-7A", "lost_exact": true, "missed_losses": [], "missed_permanent": false, "pair_id": "DB-7", "perm_flag_consistent": true, "phantom_losses": [], "pred_permanent": true, "raw_response": "<think>\nWe are given a command to run and then a restore script. We need to predict the outcome in terms of lost resources, recoverable resources, and whether there is permanent loss.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the command to be run:\n\n```sql\nsqlite3 -bail app.db \"PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON; BEGIN; INSERT OR REPLACE INTO contacts VALUES (900, 'desk45@example.invalid', 'C900', 'west', '2026-07-01'); COMMIT;\"\n```\n\nThis command:\n- Turns on foreign keys (PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON)\n- Starts a transaction (BEGIN)\n- Does an `INSERT OR REPLACE` into the `contacts` table for a new row with id=900.\n\nNote: The `INSERT OR REPLACE` statement in SQLite works based on UNIQUE or PRIMARY KEY constraints. If a row with the same primary key or unique index already exists, it will delete the existing row and insert the new one. Otherwise, it inserts a new row.\n\nLooking at the schema for the `contacts` table:\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE contacts (\n    id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY,\n    email TEXT NOT NULL,\n    tag TEXT NOT NULL,\n    region TEXT NOT NULL,\n    joined TEXT NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX contact_key ON contacts(email);\n```\n\nThere are two constraints: the primary key on `id` and a unique index on `email`.\n\nThe row we are inserting has:\n  id = 900\n  email = 'desk45@example.invalid'\n\nNow, let's see if there's a conflict:\n\nFrom the provided data for `contacts`:\n\nWe have a row with id=45 that has email='desk45@example.invalid'. Therefore:\n- The unique index on email will cause a conflict because 'desk45@example.invalid' already exists.\n\nBut note: the `INSERT OR REPLACE` will resolve the conflict by replacing the existing row that caused the conflict. Since the conflict is on the email unique index, the row with id=45 will be deleted and the new row (id=900) will be inserted.\n\nHowever, note the foreign key constraint: the `assignments` table has a foreign key `contact_id` that references `contacts(id)` with `ON DELETE CASCADE`.\n\nSo, when the row with id=45 is deleted (due to the replace), all assignments that have `contact_id=45` will be automatically deleted (because of the cascade).\n\nNow, what about the row with id=900? It has the same email as the old row (id=45) but a different id and other fields.\n\nAfter the command, the `contacts` table will have the new row (id=900) and the old row (id=45) will be gone.\n\nAlso, because of the cascade, any assignments referencing id=45 will be deleted.\n\nLet's check the assignments table:\n\nWe have:\n- Row 301: contact_id=45 -> deleted\n- Row 302: contact_id=45 -> deleted\n- Row 303: contact_id=42 -> remains\n- Row 304: contact_id=47 -> remains\n\nTherefore, assignments 301 and 302 (R9) are lost.\n\nNow, what about the `contacts_snapshot` table? It has a row with id=45 (R8). But note that the command does not touch the `contacts_snapshot` table. So that row remains.\n\nAlso, the `project_codes` table is not touched.\n\nNow, let's look at the restore script:\n\n```sql\nsqlite3 app.db \"PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON; DELETE FROM contacts WHERE id=900; INSERT OR IGNORE INTO contacts SELECT * FROM contacts_snapshot;\" || true\n```\n\nThis script:\n1. Turns on foreign keys.\n2. Deletes the row in `contacts` with id=900.\n3. Inserts rows from `contacts_snapshot` but only if they don't cause a unique constraint violation (using OR IGNORE).\n\nNow, what is in `contacts_snapshot`? From the data:\n\nOnly one row: id=45, email='desk45@example.invalid', etc.\n\nAfter the command we ran, the `contacts` table has:\n- The original rows except id=45 (which was replaced by id=900) and then we are going to delete id=900.\n\nAfter the delete of id=900, the `contacts` table will have the same rows as before the command, except without id=45 and without id=900.\n\nThen we do: `INSERT OR IGNORE INTO contacts SELECT * FROM contacts_snapshot;`\n\nThe `contacts_snapshot` has the row for id=45. Now, when inserting:\n- The primary key id=45: currently there is no row with id=45 in `contacts` (we deleted the original and then inserted 900, then deleted 900, so id=45 is free).\n- The email='desk45@example.invalid': we must check the unique index. In the current state, there is no row with that email (because we deleted the row with id=900 which had that email). So there is no conflict.\n\nTherefore, the row from the snapshot (id=45) will be inserted without any problem.\n\nBut note: what about the assignments that were referencing id=45? They were deleted by the cascade when we replaced id=45 with id=900. When we insert id=45 again, the assignments (301 and 302) are not automatically recovered because they were deleted.\n\nThe restore script only operates on the `contacts` table (deleting id=900 and inserting from the snapshot). It does not touch the `assignments` table.\n\nTherefore, the assignments that were lost (301 and 302) remain lost even after the restore.\n\nNow, let's map the resources:\n\nR1: contacts row 41 -> not affected (remains)\nR2: assignments row 303 -> remains (contact_id=42, which was not touched)\nR3: contacts row 45 -> lost initially (replaced) but then restored by the restore script? \n   - After the command: row 45 is gone (replaced by 900).\n   - After the restore: row 45 is reinserted from the snapshot. And the row has the same content as originally? \n     The contacts_snapshot row for id=45 is the same as the original contacts row? \n     From the data: \n        Original: 45,'desk45@example.invalid','C45','east','2026-01-01'\n        Snapshot: 45,'desk45@example.invalid','C45','east','2026-01-01'\n     So the content is the same.\n\nR4: contacts row 47 -> remains\nR5: project_codes row 1 -> remains\nR6: contacts row 42 -> remains\nR7: assignments row 304 -> remains (contact_id=47, not touched)\nR8: contacts_snapshot row 45 -> remains (the command didn't touch this table) and then used by restore\nR9: assignments rows 301, 302 -> lost because of cascade and not restored.\n\nSo which resources are lost right after the command?\n- R3: contacts row 45 (replaced by 900, so gone)\n- R9: assignments rows 301 and 302 (deleted by cascade)\n\nBut note: R3 is a row in `contacts` that is lost. R9 is two rows in `assignments`. However, the resource IDs are given as R9 for assignments rows 301 and 302. So we represent R9 as a single resource.\n\nSimilarly, each row in the resources is individually listed? Actually, the resources are:\n  R1, R2, R3, R4, R5, R6, R7, R8, R9.\n\nSo:\n- Lost: R3 (contacts row 45) and R9 (assignments rows 301, 302) -> [\"R3\", \"R9\"]\n\nNow, recoverable: the subset of lost that the restore script brings back with exactly the original content.\n\nAfter the restore script:\n- R3 (contacts row 45) is reinserted from the snapshot and has the same content -> recoverable.\n- R9 (assignments rows 301 and 302) are not restored by the script -> not recoverable.\n\nTherefore, recoverable: [\"R3\"]\n\nPermanent loss: true because R9 is lost and not recovered.\n\nBut note: the restore script runs `DELETE FROM contacts WHERE id=900` and then inserts from the snapshot. The deletion of id=900 does not cause any cascade because there are no assignments referencing id=900? Why? Because we just inserted id=900 and then immediately after, we run the restore script. And we did not assign any assignments to id=900. So no cascade on that delete.\n\nTherefore, the outcome:\n\nlost: [\"R3\", \"R9\"]\nrecoverable: [\"R3\"]\npermanent_loss: true\n\nBut note: the resource R9 is a single resource that represents two rows. However, the question says \"IDs of resources\", and R9 is one ID. So we list R9 as one lost resource.\n\nAlso, note that the restore scri