nly concerned with these resources.\n\nNow, what happens to the resources?\n\n- R5: folders row 70 -> This row is directly deleted. So it is lost.\n- R4: documents rows 401,402 -> These have folder_id=70. They will be updated: folder_id becomes NULL. But the content (title, revision, checksum) remains the same? The resource R4 is about the row content? Note: the resource says \"content\", but we have to see what is stored. The resource ID refers to the row? The row still exists but with folder_id changed? \n\nThe problem says: \"lost\" means \"whose content is gone or changed\". So changing a value (like setting folder_id to NULL) counts as changed.\n\nSimilarly:\n- R9: documents row 403 -> same as R4? It also has folder_id=70.\n\nBut note: R4 is defined as \"documents rows 401,402\" and R9 as \"documents row 403\". So:\n\n- R4: rows 401 and 402 -> both will have folder_id set to NULL -> changed.\n- R9: row 403 -> folder_id set to NULL -> changed.\n\nNow, what about the other resources?\n\n- R1: documents row 404 -> folder_id=71. Not affected by deleting folder 70? So unchanged.\n- R2: folders row 71 -> exists? Not deleted. Unchanged.\n- R3: documents row 406 -> folder_id=NULL. Not affected. Unchanged.\n- R6: documents row 405 -> folder_id=72. Not affected. Unchanged.\n\nBut wait, what about the cascade on `folder_checks`? We don't have any resource defined for `folder_checks`. So even if rows in `folder_checks` are deleted, they are not in our resource list. Similarly, `folder_labels` rows set to NULL are not in our resources.\n\nSo the immediate loss/changed resources are: R5 (deleted), R4 (changed), R9 (changed).\n\nBut note: R5 is a row in `folders` and R4 and R9 are rows in `documents`.\n\nNow, the restore script:\n\nsqlite3 app.db \"PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON; INSERT OR IGNORE INTO folders SELECT * FROM folders_snapshot; INSERT OR IGNORE INTO documents SELECT * FROM documents_snapshot;\" || true\n\nThis script:\n1. Turns on foreign keys.\n2. Inserts rows from `folders_snapshot` into `folders` with `OR IGNORE` (so if a row with the same primary key exists, it ignores).\n3. Inserts rows from `documents_snapshot` into `documents` with `OR IGNORE`.\n\nNow, what is in the snapshots?\n\nFrom the provided data:\n\nfolders_snapshot has only one row: (70, 'intake', 'desk-a', 'north')\ndocuments_snapshot has three rows: (401,70,'invoice',2,'c401'), (402,70,'receipt',1,'c402'), (403,70,'memo',4,'c403')\n\nWhat happens when we run the restore?\n\n- For `folders`: \n  The snapshot row (id=70) is inserted. But we just deleted the row with id=70? So it doesn't exist anymore? Therefore, the INSERT will succeed? But note: the folders table might have other rows (71,72) but they are not in the snapshot. The restore script only inserts from the snapshot.\n\n  However, the `OR IGNORE` clause: if a row with the same primary key (id=70) already exists, it would skip. But after the delete, there is no row with id=70. So it inserts the snapshot row. Therefore, R5 (folders row 70) is restored to its original state? But note: the folders row 70 in the snapshot is exactly the same as the original? We see the same data: 'intake','desk-a','north'\n\n- For `documents`:\n  The snapshot has rows 401,402,403. But note: these rows exist in the documents table? Right after the command, they exist but with folder_id set to NULL. So when we try to insert from the snapshot, the primary key (id) already exists? Therefore, because of `OR IGNORE`, these rows will be skipped.\n\n  Therefore, the documents rows 401,402,403 remain with folder_id=NULL? They are not restored to the original state (which had folder_id=70).\n\nBut wait: the restore script does not update existing rows? It only inserts new rows or ignores if exists. So the rows 401,402,403 are still present with folder_id=NULL? They are not restored.\n\nTherefore:\n- R5 (folders row 70) is restored exactly? Yes, because it is inserted from the snapshot and the row was missing after deletion.\n- R4 (documents rows 401,402) and R9 (documents row 403) are not restored? Because the restore script doesn't update them; it only tries to insert and ignores because the primary keys already exist.\n\nSo:\n- R5 is recoverable? Yes, it is restored exactly.\n- R4 and R9 are lost and not recoverable by the restore script? Because they remain changed (folder_id=NULL) and the restore script doesn't update them.\n\nBut note: what if the foreign key constraint prevents the insertion of the snapshot row in `folders`? Because we are inserting a row with id=70 and there are rows in `documents` (401,402,403) that reference folder_id=70? But wait: after the command, these rows have folder_id=NULL? So when we insert folders row 70, there is no immediate violation? And then when we try to insert the documents_snapshot rows, they are ignored? So no foreign key violation.\n\nTherefore, the lost resources right after the command are: R5 (gone), R4 (changed), R9 (changed). So lost: R5, R4, R9.\n\nAfter the restore script:\n- R5 is recovered (so it's in recoverable)\n- R4 and R9 are still changed? They are not recovered.\n\nBut note: the resource R4 is defined as a group of two rows? And we are to list the resource IDs? So R4 and R9 are separate.\n\nSo:\nlost: [\"R4\", \"R5\", \"R9\"]\nrecoverable: [\"R5\"]   (because only R5 is restored to original)\npermanent_loss: true   (because R4 and R9 are lost and not recoverable)\n\nBut wait: what about R7 and R8? They are snapshot rows. Are they considered? The problem defines:\n\n\"lost\": IDs of resources whose content is gone or changed right after the command.\n\nBut R7 and R8 are from the snapshot tables? The command doesn't touch the snapshot tables. So they are unchanged? Therefore, not lost.\n\nSimilarly, R1, R2, R3, R6 are not affected.\n\nTherefore, we don't include them in \"lost\".\n\nBut note: R7 and R8 are not part of the main tables? They are in the snapshot tables. The resources are defined by the given IDs. So we only care about the resources as defined.\n\nNow, is there any other effect? For example, the cascade delete on `folder_checks`? We don't have any resource defined for `folder_checks`, so we don't worry.\n\nTherefore:\nlost: R4, R5, R9\nrecoverable: R5\npermanent_loss: true (because R4 and R9 are not recovered)\n\nBut note: the resource R4 is \"documents rows 401,402\". We have to represent it as a single resource ID? That is, \"R4\" refers to both rows? So when we say R4 is lost, we mean both rows? Similarly, the restore script fails to restore both? So we list \"R4\" as lost and not recoverable.\n\nSimilarly, R9 is one row.\n\nSo the answer should be:\n\n{\n  \"lost\": [\"R4\", \"R5\", \"R9\"],\n  \"recoverable\": [\"R5\"],\n  \"permanent_loss\": true\n}\n\nBut note: the example format uses an array of strings exactly as the resource IDs. So we write \"R4\", \"R5\", \"R9\".\n\nLet me double-check the resources:\n\n- R4: documents rows 401,402 -> after command: folder_id becomes NULL -> changed? So lost.\n- R5: folders row 70 -> deleted -> lost.\n- R9: documents row 403 -> folder_id becomes NULL -> changed -> lost.\n\nRestore:\n- R5: inserted from snapshot -> back to original -> recoverable.\n- R4 and R9: remain changed -> not recoverable.\n\nThus, permanent loss because R4 and R9 are lost and not recoverable.\n\nFinal answer in JSON.\n</think>\n{\"lost\": [\"R4\", \"R5\", \"R9\"], \"recoverable\": [\"R5\"], \"permanent_loss\": true}", "rec_exact": true}
