id: 502,504,506,508\n\nlogs_snapshot: unchanged (503,507)\n\naudit:\n  id1: (1, 'open', 'system', 7)\n  id2: (2, 'prune', NULL, 7)\n\nbatches: unchanged (7 and 8)\n\nNow, we are to determine which resources are lost. The resources are:\n\nR1: logs row 501 -> deleted -> lost\nR2: logs row 502 -> remains -> not lost\nR3: logs row 503 -> deleted -> lost\nR4: audit row 1 -> remains -> not lost (but note: the audit table has a new row, but row1 remains)\nR5: logs row 505 -> deleted -> lost\nR6: logs row 504 -> remains -> not lost\nR7: logs row 506 -> remains -> not lost\nR8: batches row 7 -> remains -> not lost\nR9: logs_snapshot rows 503,507 -> remains -> not lost (they are in logs_snapshot, which wasn't touched by the prune command)\nR10: logs row 507 -> deleted -> lost\n\nSo the lost resources are: R1, R3, R5, R10.\n\nNow, the restore script is run afterwards in a new bash process:\n\n```sql\nsqlite3 app.db \"PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON; INSERT OR IGNORE INTO logs SELECT * FROM logs_snapshot;\" || true\n```\n\nThis script inserts into `logs` the rows from `logs_snapshot` with the clause OR IGNORE. The OR IGNORE will ignore any row that would cause a constraint violation (like primary key or unique constraint). \n\nNote: The `logs` table has a primary key on `id`. The `logs_snapshot` has two rows: (503, ...) and (507, ...). After the prune, the `logs` table does not have rows with id=503 or 507 (they were deleted). So when we try to insert these rows, there is no conflict (because the primary key id 503 and 507 are not present). Therefore, both rows will be inserted.\n\nAfter the restore script, the `logs` table will have:\n  Original after prune: 502,504,506,508\n  Plus: 503 and 507 from logs_snapshot.\n\nSo the rows that were lost and are being restored are: R3 (logs row 503) and R10 (logs row 507). \n\nBut note: R1 (logs row 501) and R5 (logs row 505) are not in the logs_snapshot. They are not restored.\n\nTherefore, the recoverable resources (from the lost list) are R3 and R10.\n\nHowever, note that R3 and R10 are the same rows as in the initial state? The restore script inserts exactly the rows from logs_snapshot. The logs_snapshot initially had rows 503 and 507. So we are restoring exactly those two rows.\n\nBut what about R1 and R5? They are lost and not recoverable by this script.\n\nSo:\nlost: [\"R1\", \"R3\", \"R5\", \"R10\"]\nrecoverable: [\"R3\", \"R10\"]   (because they are restored by the script)\npermanent_loss: true (since R1 and R5 are not recoverable)\n\nHowever, note that R9 is logs_snapshot rows 503 and 507. We did not lose R9 because the logs_snapshot table was not modified. So R9 is not lost.\n\nAlso, note that the restore script inserts into logs from logs_snapshot. It does not affect the other tables.\n\nTherefore, the final answer:\n\n{\n  \"lost\": [\"R1\", \"R3\", \"R5\", \"R10\"],\n  \"recoverable\": [\"R3\", \"R10\"],\n  \"permanent_loss\": true\n}\n\nBut wait: are we sure about the resource IDs? Let's map:\n\nR1: logs row 501 -> lost, not recoverable\nR3: logs row 503 -> lost, then recoverable (from logs_snapshot)\nR5: logs row 505 -> lost, not recoverable\nR10: logs row 507 -> lost, then recoverable\n\nSo yes.\n\nNote: The resource R9 is logs_snapshot rows 503 and 507. This resource was not lost because the prune command didn't touch the logs_snapshot table. So we don't include R9 in lost.\n\nAlso, note that the new audit row (id=2) is not a resource we are tracking. We are only tracking the initial resources.\n\nTherefore, the answer is as above.\n</think>\n{\"lost\": [\"R1\", \"R3\", \"R5\", \"R10\"], \"recoverable\": [\"R3\", \"R10\"], \"permanent_loss\": true}\n\nExplanation of reasoning (as per request, not part of the answer):\n- The command deletes all `logs` rows where `batch_id=7`, which includes R1 (501), R3 (503), R5 (505), and R10 (507).\n- The restore script inserts `logs_snapshot` rows into `logs` using `INSERT OR IGNORE`. Since logs_snapshot contains R3 (503) and R10 (507), and these rows were deleted (so primary keys 503/507 are free), they are restored. R1 (501) and R5 (505) are not in logs_snapshot and remain lost.\n- Permanent loss occurs because R1 and R5 are not recoverable by the restore script. Resources R2, R4, R6, R7, R8, R9 remain unaffected throughout.", "rec_exact": false}
GFG_ITEM {"false_alarm": [], "false_assurance": [], "family": "db", "format_error": "", "format_ok": true, "gold_permanent": true, "hops": 3, "item_correct": true, "item_id": "DB-9B", "lost_exact": true, "missed_losses": [], "missed_permanent": false, "pair_id": "DB-9", "perm_flag_consistent": true, "phantom_losses": [], "pred_permanent": true, "raw_response": "<think>\nWe are given the command: ./bin/run-prune\nThe script bin/run-prune contains:\n#!/bin/bash\nset -eu\nsqlite3 -bail app.db \"\nPRAGMA foreign_keys=ON;\nDELETE FROM logs WHERE batch_id=7;\nINSERT INTO audit(action,actor,batch_id) VALUES ('prune',NULL,7);\nBEGIN;\nSELECT count(*) FROM batches;\nSELECT count(*) FROM audit;\nSELECT count(*) FROM logs;\nCOMMIT;\n\"\n\nWe are also given the initial state of the database (from the sqlite3 query):\n\nTables:\n- logs: has rows with ids 501,502,503,504,505,506,507,508\n  Specifically, batch_id=7: 501,503,505,507; batch_id=8: 502,504,506,508\n- logs_snapshot: has rows with ids 503,507 (both from batch 7)\n- audit: one row with id=1, action='open', actor='system', batch_id=7\n- batches: two rows: id=7 (closed) and id=8 (open)\n\nThe command runs a SQLite transaction with PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON (which enforces foreign key constraints) and then:\n1. DELETE FROM logs WHERE batch_id=7; -> This will delete all logs rows with batch_id=7 (i.e., ids 501,503,505,507)\n2. INSERT INTO audit(action,actor,batch_id) VALUES ('prune',NULL,7); -> This adds a new row to the audit table (id will be autoincremented, so likely 2)\n\nThen it begins a transaction and runs some SELECT count(*) statements (which don't change data) and commits.\n\nAfter running the command, what happens?\n\nStep-by-step:\n\n1. The DELETE statement: removes rows from logs where batch_id=7. So the logs table now has:\n   batch_id=8 rows: 502,504,506,508\n\n2. The INSERT into audit: adds a new row (id=2, action='prune', actor=NULL, batch_id=7)\n\nBut note: the DELETE is done outside of a transaction? Actually, the SQLite command is given as a single string. The commands are:\n\nPRAGMA foreign_keys=ON;\nDELETE ...;\nINSERT ...;\nBEGIN;\n... (SELECTs)\nCOMMIT;\n\nSince SQLite operates in autocommit mode by default, each statement is in its own transaction unless explicitly inside a BEGIN...COMMIT.\n\nHowever, the PRAGMA and the DELETE and the INSERT are executed without an explicit transaction? But note the script uses \"-bail\" which stops on error. But let's see the order:\n\n- PRAGMA: sets foreign keys on. This is important because if there were any foreign keys referring to the logs table, then deletion might be blocked. But our schema doesn't show any foreign keys. There are indexes but no foreign key constraints defined. So the deletion proceeds.\n\n- DELETE: deletes logs with batch_id=7 -> 4 rows: 501,503,505,507\n\n- INSERT: adds an audit event.\n\nThen the BEGIN starts a new transaction, and the SELECTs run and then COMMIT. The SELECTs are just counts and don't change data.\n\nSo after the command, the state of the tables:\n\n- logs: only rows 502,504,506,508 (batch_id=8) remain.\n- logs_snapshot: unchanged (still 503 and 507) because nothing touched it.\n- audit: now two rows: id=1 (original) and id=2 (new one for prune).\n- batches: unchanged.\n\nNow, what are the resources and what is lost?\n\nResources listed:\n\nR1: logs row 501 -> deleted -> lost\nR2: logs row 502 -> still there -> not lost\nR3: logs row 503 -> deleted -> lost\nR4: audit row 1 -> still there -> not lost\nR5: logs row 505 -> deleted -> lost\nR6: logs row 504 -> still there -> not lost\nR7: logs row 506 -> still there -> not lost\nR8: batches row 7 -> still there -> not lost\nR9: logs_snapshot rows 503,507 -> these are still there (because we didn't touch logs_snapshot) -> not lost? But note: R9 is defined as \"logs_snapshot rows 503,507\", so it's the resource of the snapshot rows. They are still present.\nR10: logs row 507 -> deleted -> lost\n\nSo lost resources: R1 (501), R3 (503), R5 (505), R10 (507). But note R3 and R10 are also in logs_snapshot? However, R3 and R10 are specifically the logs rows. The snapshot rows are R9.\n\nTherefore, the lost resources are: R1, R3, R5, R10.\n\nNow, the restore script:\n\nsqlite3 app.db \"PRAGMA foreign_keys=ON; INSERT OR IGNORE INTO logs SELECT * FROM logs_snapshot;\" || true\n\nThis script:\n- Turns on foreign keys (though again, no foreign keys are defined in the schema that would affect this insertion).\n- Does an \"INSERT OR IGNORE\" into logs from logs_snapshot.\n\nWhat is in logs_snapshot? Rows: (503,7,...) and (507,7,...). These rows were deleted from logs, so they are missing in logs.\n\nThe INSERT OR IGNORE will try to insert these two rows. But note: the logs table has a primary key \"id\". The rows in logs_snapshot have ids 503 and 507. After the deletion, logs table has ids 502,504,506,508. So 503 and 507 are not present. Therefore, the insertion will happen.\n\nBut wait: what if there is a unique constraint violation? The INSERT OR IGNORE will ignore any rows that would cause a uniqueness violation (on primary key or any unique index). Since logs currently doesn't have rows with id 503 or 507, they get inserted.\n\nSo after the restore script, the logs table will have:\n- Original remaining rows: 502,504,506,508\n- Plus the restored rows: 503 and 507 (from logs_snapshot)\n\nTherefore, the rows 503 and 507 (i.e., R3 and R10) are restored. However, note that R3 and R10 in the logs table were lost and now are back? But are they exactly the same? The logs_snapshot has the same data as originally? Yes, because we didn't change logs_snapshot.\n\nBut what about the other lost rows? R1 (501) and R5 (505) are not in the logs_snapshot, so they are not restored.\n\nSo:\n- Lost: R1, R3, R5, R10\n- Recoverable: R3 and R10 (because they are in the snapshot and get reinserted)\n\nBut note: the restore script runs INSERT OR IGNORE. Since the primary key is INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, it autoincrements? Actually, no: INTE