erpreted by a custom script? However, the command we are running is just `git stash`. \n\n   Actually, the environment variable `STASH_MODE` is set but not used by the standard `git stash` command. The standard `git stash` without any arguments stashes changes to tracked files and also stages (if any). It does not stash untracked files by default unless `-u` is used. So in this case, only the modified tracked file (`data/ledger.csv`) will be stashed. The untracked file (`data/queue.csv`) is not touched.\n\n   After `git stash`:\n     - The working directory is reset to the HEAD for the tracked file `data/ledger.csv` (so it goes back to the state before the change, i.e., with 24).\n     - The stash is created with the change we had in `data/ledger.csv`. The stash is stored in the stash list.\n\n   Now, look at `git status --short` again: the untracked file `data/queue.csv` is still present.\n\n2. `git clean -fd notes/`:\n   This command removes untracked files and directories in the `notes/` directory. Since `-f` forces removal and `-d` removes directories, it will clean the `notes/` directory.\n\n   What is in the `notes/` directory? We don't have a full listing, but the resources provided include:\n     R1: notes/check-03.txt\n     R2: notes/check-05.txt\n     R3: notes/check-17.txt\n     R4: .gitignore\n     R5: config/clean.env\n     R6: notes/check-29.txt\n     R7: notes/check-31.txt\n     R8: notes/check-51.txt\n     R9: data/queue.csv\n     R10: data/ledger.csv\n\n   So the `notes/` directory contains at least R1, R2, R3, R6, R7, R8. Note that the resource R4 (.gitignore) and R5 (config/clean.env) are not under `notes/`.\n\n   Since `git clean -fd notes/` removes untracked files and directories in `notes/`, and given that the `git status` shows no untracked files under `notes/`? Wait, let me check the `git status --short` output:\n\n      M data/ledger.csv\n      ?? data/queue.csv\n\n   There is no mention of any untracked files under `notes/`. Therefore, we m